# Objective 1: coverage of the "pathway -> enzyme -> gene" legs of the
# chain. Within the genuine (P2410) pathways linked to compounds found in
# each of the 6 species from query 03, how many of the pathway's P527
# parts are actually typed as an enzyme (Q8047), and of those, how many
# have an `encoded by` (P702) gene link?
#
# Run at https://query.wikidata.org/. Result (run 2026-11-21): across ALL
# SIX species combined, only 3 genuine pathways contain any item typed as
# an enzyme at all, and there is exactly ONE distinct enzyme-typed item in
# total: "M-phase inducer phosphatase" (Q3775177) - a human cell-cycle
# enzyme, unrelated to plant biosynthesis. It has ZERO `encoded by` (P702)
# gene link.
#
# Reading: the pathway -> enzyme -> gene legs of the chain aren't just
# sparse for plants - for the pathways actually reachable from
# plant-found compounds, they are effectively empty. This is consistent
# with queries 01/03's finding that the "genes" attached to these
# pathways via P527 are typically typed directly as genes (Q7187), not
# reached via a distinct enzyme entity at all - i.e. the
# pathway -P527-> enzyme -P702-> gene chain the project proposal
# describes mostly doesn't exist in the data; what exists instead is a
# flatter (and non-taxon-specific - see the gap analysis) pathway -P527-> gene link.

SELECT (COUNT(DISTINCT ?pathway) AS ?realPathwaysWithAnyEnzyme)
       (COUNT(DISTINCT ?enzyme) AS ?enzymeTypedParts)
WHERE {
  VALUES ?taxon {
    wd:Q158695   # Arabidopsis thaliana
    wd:Q101815   # Camellia sinensis (tea)
    wd:Q47685    # Coffea arabica (coffee)
    wd:Q42385    # Theobroma cacao (cacao)
    wd:Q146212   # Brassica oleracea
    wd:Q3384     # Brassica rapa
  }
  ?compound wdt:P703 ?taxon .
  ?pathway wdt:P2410 ?wpid .
  ?pathway wdt:P527 ?compound .
  ?pathway wdt:P527 ?enzyme .
  ?enzyme wdt:P31 wd:Q8047 .   # instance of enzyme
}

